= 1
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

The weight is strictly positive and all its <polynomial> moments are finite. Thus the given integral is a <inner product>: for a nonzero <polynomial>, its square is positive on a nonempty interval, so its weighted integral is strictly positive.

In the <finite-dimensional vector space> $V_n$, subtract the <orthogonal projection onto a finite-dimensional subspace> of $x^n$ onto $V_{n-1}$:
$$
\phi_n=x^n-\operatorname{proj}_{V_{n-1}}x^n.
$$
This is monic, since the subtracted <polynomial> has lower degree, and is orthogonal to every lower-degree <polynomial>. If $\phi_n,\psi_n$ were two such <monic polynomials>, their difference would lie in $V_{n-1}$ and be orthogonal to that space. Taking its <inner product> with itself gives zero, hence the difference vanishes. This proves existence and uniqueness for every $n$, with $\phi_0=1$.

Odd moments vanish because the weight is even; the even moments are
$$
\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-|x|}x^{2k}dx
=2\int_0^\infty e^{-x}x^{2k}dx=2(2k)!.
$$
Consequently $\phi_1=x$, while a monic quadratic orthogonal to $x$ has no linear term, and orthogonality to one gives its constant term $-4/2=-2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\phi_0=1,\qquad\phi_1=x,\qquad\phi_2=x^2-2.}
$$
Finally $(-1)^n\phi_n(-x)$ is also monic and orthogonal to every lower-degree <polynomial>, by the change of variable $x\mapsto-x$. Uniqueness implies $\phi_n(-x)=(-1)^n\phi_n(x)$. In particular $\phi_{2007}$ is odd and
$$
\boxed{[x^{2000}]\phi_{2007}=0.}
$$
This is <parity of monic orthogonal polynomials for an even weight>.

= 2
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}
{c}

Use the <monomial basis> $e_k=x^{n-k}y^k$, $0\leq k\leq n$. Direct differentiation gives
$$
Pe_k=k e_{k-1},\qquad Qe_k=(n-k)e_{k+1},
$$
with the endpoint terms zero. Repeated application therefore gives $P^{n+1}=Q^{n+1}=0$, while $P^ne_n=n!e_0\neq0$ and $Q^ne_0=n!e_n\neq0$. Each <nilpotency index> is exactly $n+1$, so
$$
\boxed{m_P(t)=m_Q(t)=t^{n+1}.}
$$
Next
$$
PQe_k=(n-k)(k+1)e_k,\qquad
QPe_k=k(n-k+1)e_k,
$$
and hence $(PQ-QP)e_k=(n-2k)e_k$. These $n+1$ real <eigenvalues> are distinct. A <polynomial> annihilates this operator exactly when it vanishes at all of them, giving
$$
\boxed{m_{PQ-QP}(t)=\prod_{k=0}^n(t-(n-2k)).}
$$
The <commutator> is diagonal in the displayed <monomial basis>. Since $n\geq1$, $P,Q$ are nonzero <nilpotent linear map>; a <diagonalizable> nilpotent map would have a <diagonal matrix> with all <eigenvalues> zero and thus be the zero map. Therefore **only $PQ-QP$ is <diagonalizable>**. The calculation realizes <raising and lowering on binary homogeneous polynomials>.

= 3
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}
{c}

For $z=a+b\sqrt d$, let $\bar z=a-b\sqrt d$ denote the <algebraic conjugate>, not necessarily ordinary complex conjugation when $d>0$. Conjugation respects multiplication, and $z\bar z=a^2-db^2$. Therefore
$$
N(zz')=|(zz')\overline{zz'}|
=|z\bar z|\,|z'\bar z'|
=\boxed{N(z)N(z')}.
$$
If $z$ is a <unit in a ring>, choose its inverse $w$ in the same <ring>. Then $N(z)N(w)=N(1)=1$, and both norms are nonnegative <integers>, forcing $N(z)=1$. Conversely, if $N(z)=1$, then $z\bar z=\pm1$, so $z^{-1}=\pm\bar z$ belongs to the <ring>. Thus **$z$ is a <unit in a ring> exactly when $N(z)=1$**.

For negative $d$, the equation becomes $a^2+|d|b^2=1$. If $d=-1$, its <integer> solutions give the four <units> $\pm1,\pm i$. If $d<-1$, the term $|d|b^2$ forces $b=0$, leaving $a=\pm1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb Z[\sqrt d]^\times=
\begin{cases}\{1,-1,i,-i\},&d=-1,\\
\{1,-1\},&d<-1.\end{cases}}
$$
For $d=2$, the element $u=1+\sqrt2$ has <field norm> $|1-2|=1$. Its powers $u^n$, $n\geq0$, are all <units> by <multiplicative function> and are distinct, since their positive real values strictly increase with $n$. Thus $\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]$ has infinitely many <units>. This argument concerns the stated integer-coefficient <ring>; it does not replace it by a larger <ring> of algebraic <integers>.

