= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The topology on $A$ is the <subspace topology> inherited from the <complex plane>. A subset $B$ is open in $A$ if $B=A\cap U$ for some <open subset> $U\subseteq\mathbb C$. Equivalently, every $b\in B$ has a radius $\epsilon>0$ such that $A\cap\{z:|z-b|<\epsilon\}\subseteq B$.

A subset $B$ is closed in $A$ if $A\setminus B$ is open in $A$, equivalently if $B=A\cap F$ for a <closed subset> $F$ of the <complex plane>. These are relative properties: the same subset need not be open or closed in the ambient plane.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The set $A$ is <disconnected space> if it has a <separation of a topological space>: two nonempty disjoint subsets $U,V$ open in $A$ with $A=U\cup V$. Each is then also closed in $A$, being the other's complement.

The set $A$ is <connected> if no such <separation of a topological space> exists. Equivalently its only subsets that are both open and closed are $\varnothing$ and $A$. A <separation of a topological space> uses openness in $A$ rather than necessarily in the entire <complex plane>.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Because the image is integer-valued, the open interval $(n-\tfrac12,n+\tfrac12)$ contains exactly one possible image value, namely $n$. Thus
$$
f^{-1}(\{n\})=f^{-1}\bigl((n-\tfrac12,n+\tfrac12)\bigr).
$$
The right side is open in $A$ by <continuity>, so
$$
\boxed{f^{-1}(\{n\})\text{ is open in }A.}
$$
This includes the possibility of an empty <fiber>. The source notation $f^{-1}(n)$ is understood as the <preimage> of the singleton $\{n\}$.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Since $A$ is nonempty, choose $a_0\in A$ and put $n_0=f(a_0)$. The <fiber> $U=f^{-1}(\{n_0\})$ is nonempty and open by part (c). Its complement is the union of the other <integer> <fibers> and is therefore also open. If the image contained a second value, that complement would be nonempty, and these two disjoint <open subsets> would separate $A$. <connectedness> excludes this. Hence
$$
\boxed{f(A)=\{n_0\}.}
$$
Equivalently a <continuous function> from a <connected> space to the discrete space of <integers> is constant. The proof uses relative openness and does not require the original subset $A$ to be open in the plane.

= 5
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}
{c}

A function on the open set $D$ is <holomorphic> if at every $a\in D$ the complex limit
$$
f'(a)=\lim_{\substack{z\to a\\z\neq a}}
\frac{f(z)-f(a)}{z-a}
$$
exists. The limit is independent of the direction of approach.

A <pole> of order $n\geq1$ at $a$ means that $f(z)=(z-a)^{-n}h(z)$ in a punctured neighborhood, where $h$ extends holomorphically across $a$ and $h(a)\neq0$. Equivalently $(z-a)^nf(z)$ has a nonzero finite limit at $a$.

For the given quotient, $\cos z=1$ is equivalent, on putting $q=e^{iz}$, to $q+q^{-1}=2$, or $(q-1)^2=0$. Thus all possible <poles> are $a=2\pi k$, $k\in\mathbb Z$, with no additional nonreal solutions. At such a point,
$$
\cos z-1=-\tfrac12(z-a)^2+O((z-a)^4).
$$
At $a\neq0$ the numerator does not vanish, so the <pole> has order two. At zero the numerator cancels one order:
$$
\frac z{\cos z-1}=-\frac2z-\frac z6+O(z^3).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{z=0\text{ is a simple pole};\quad
z=2\pi k,\ k\neq0,\text{ are double poles}.}
$$
The numerator's cancellation at zero is why not every denominator zero produces the same <pole> order.

