= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Parametrize the vertical line as $z=a+it$, $t\in\mathbb R$. Then $e^z=e^a(\cos t+i\sin t)$, whose modulus is $e^a$ and whose argument takes every value. The image is
$$
\boxed{\{w:|w|=e^a\}.}
$$
Increasing $t$ traverses the <circle> counterclockwise, repeating with period $2\pi$. There is no injectivity on the full line.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Parametrize the horizontal line as $z=t+ib$, $t\in\mathbb R$. Then $e^z=e^t e^{ib}$, and $e^t$ takes every positive real value exactly once. The image is
$$
\boxed{\{re^{ib}:r>0\}.}
$$
This is the <ray> with argument $b$ modulo $2\pi$, excluding its endpoint zero. As $t$ increases, the image moves outward from arbitrarily small to arbitrarily large radius.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Let $w=1/(z+1)$ and invert to $z=1/w-1$. The <pole> $z=-1$ lies on the excluded boundary, so it is not a point of the original region. For $w\neq0$ the first inequality becomes
$$
|z|>1
\iff|1-w|>|w|
\iff1-2\operatorname{Re}w>0.
$$
The second becomes
$$
|z-1|<2
\iff|1-2w|<2|w|
\iff1-4\operatorname{Re}w<0.
$$
Thus the image is contained in the vertical strip $1/4<\operatorname{Re}w<1/2$. Conversely every point in that strip is nonzero; its inverse satisfies both original inequalities, so no points are missing. Therefore
$$
\boxed{w(\mathcal R_1)=\{w:\tfrac14<\operatorname{Re}w<\tfrac12\}.}
$$
The two circles pass through the <pole> of the <Möbius transformation>, which is why their images are straight lines. Direct inequality transformation fixes which side of each line is required.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The <complex exponential function> gives the natural onto map:
$$
\boxed{w=e^z,\qquad e^{1/4}<|w|<e^{1/2}.}
$$
Its derivative is never zero, so it preserves angles locally. Conversely any point in this <annulus> has a logarithm with real part in $(1/4,1/2)$, proving surjectivity.

There is a terminology qualification. This map is not injective, since $e^{z+2\pi i}=e^z$ and the strip contains both points. If “conformal mapping onto” requires a <biholomorphism>, no such map exists: the strip is convex and <simply connected space>, whereas the <annulus> contains a loop of <winding number> one around zero that cannot contract inside it. A <biholomorphism> would be a <homeomorphism> and preserve this property.

Thus **the <complex exponential function> is a locally conformal covering; a <biholomorphism> is impossible**. It identifies imaginary translates by $2\pi i$. The printed request is satisfied under its local-angle-preserving convention, with the injectivity distinction made explicit. This is <exponential covering of an annulus by a vertical strip>.

\Image[2007-exponential-strip.png]
{title=The strip wraps repeatedly around an <annulus> under the <complex exponential function>}

= 7
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}
{c}

For $y=\alpha x+\beta x^2$, the derivatives are $y'=\alpha+2\beta x$ and $y''=2\beta$. Substituting gives $2\beta x^2-2x(\alpha+2\beta x)+2(\alpha x+\beta x^2)=0$. The independent solutions $x,x^2$ have <Wronskian> $x^2\neq0$ on $[1,2]$, so they form a <fundamental system of solutions>.

Divide the <inhomogeneous linear differential equation> by $x^2$ and use <variation of parameters>. Choosing integrals from one gives the particular solution
$$
z_p(x)=-x\int_1^x\frac{f(t)}{t^2}dt
+x^2\int_1^x\frac{f(t)}{t^3}dt.
$$
Thus $z=Ax+Bx^2+z_p$. The first <boundary condition> gives $A=-B$. The integral endpoint terms cancel when differentiating $z_p$, so the second gives
$$
0=3B-\int_1^2\frac{f(t)}{t^2}dt
+4\int_1^2\frac{f(t)}{t^3}dt.
$$
Consequently
$$
z(x)=\frac{x^2-x}{3}\int_1^2
\left(\frac1{t^2}-\frac4{t^3}\right)f(t)dt
-x\int_1^x\frac{f(t)}{t^2}dt
+x^2\int_1^x\frac{f(t)}{t^3}dt.
$$
Collect the coefficient of $f(t)$ on either side of $t=x$. A suitable <Green function> is
$$
\boxed{G(x,t)=\frac1{3t^3}
\begin{cases}
(x^2-x)(t-4),&x\leq t,\\
(t-1)(x^2-4x),&t\leq x.
\end{cases}}
$$
The two pieces agree at $x=t$, $G(1,t)=0$, and $G_x(2,t)=0$ for $t<2$. For an interior source point its derivative jump is
$$
G_x(t+,t)-G_x(t-,t)=\frac1{t^2}.
$$
Thus multiplication by the leading coefficient $t^2$ gives <unit in a ring> source strength. Away from $x=t$, both pieces solve the <homogeneous linear differential equation>. With continuous $f$, the integral representation has the required second derivative and solves the equation pointwise in $(1,2)$.

For uniqueness, the difference of two solutions is $Ax+Bx^2$. Its <boundary conditions> give $A+B=0$ and $A+4B=0$, forcing $A=B=0$. Hence **the solution is unique**. This is the <mixed Green function for an Euler operator with exponents one and two>.

= 8
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}
{c}

Choose characteristic parameter $t$ with initial value at $t=0$. The characteristic equations are
$$
\dot x=x,\qquad\dot y=2y,\qquad\dot z=xy,
\qquad(x,y,z)(0)=(s,s,s^2).
$$
The first two integrate immediately; substitution into the third gives
$$
\boxed{x=se^t,\qquad y=se^{2t},\qquad
z=\frac{s^2}{3}(2+e^{3t}),\quad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Eliminating $t$ gives the projected parabola
$$
\boxed{y=\frac{x^2}{s},\qquad x/s>0.}
$$
The sign restriction selects the half-parabola actually reached from the initial point.

The projection map $(s,t)\mapsto(se^t,se^{2t})$ has <Jacobian determinant> $se^{3t}\neq0$, so the initial curve is noncharacteristic for every allowed $s$. Its global inverse is
$$
s=\frac{x^2}{y},\qquad t=\log(y/x),
$$
defined precisely when $x,y$ have the same nonzero sign. Every such point is reached by exactly one characteristic. Hence the region of unique determination from these initial data projects onto
$$
\boxed{\{(x,y):xy>0\},}
$$
the first and third quadrants. The axes are not reached for finite parameter from $s\neq0$, and the other two quadrants have no <method of characteristics> issued from this initial curve.

As a direct check, substitution of the inverse parameters gives the solution
$$
z(x,y)=\frac{2x^4}{3y^2}+\frac{xy}{3}
\quad(xy>0).
$$
It has $z(s,s)=s^2$, and differentiating gives $xz_x+2yz_y=xy$. This is <quadrant uniqueness for unequal dilation characteristics>.

\Image[2007-characteristic-quadrants.png]
{title=Each initial point determines one half-parabola in the first or third quadrant}

= 9
{parent=2007}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}
{c}

The second <equilibrium point> equation gives $y=-(x+1)/2$. Substituting into the first gives $x+e^{(x-1)/2}=0$, so $x<0$. Put $x=-a$, with $a>0$. Then
$$
a=e^{-(a+1)/2}
\iff ae^{(a+1)/2}=1,\qquad y=(a-1)/2.
$$
The function $h(a)=ae^{(a+1)/2}$ is strictly increasing on $a\geq0$, since $h'(a)=e^{(a+1)/2}(1+a/2)>0$. It starts at zero and tends to infinity, so it takes the value one exactly once. Moreover $h(1)=e>1$, so $0<a<1$. This proves uniqueness of the stated <equilibrium point>.

At that point $e^{x+y}=a$, and the <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}1+a&a\\1&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its trace is $3+a$ and determinant $2+a$. The characteristic discriminant is $(3+a)^2-4(2+a)=(a+1)^2$, so its two <eigenvalues> are
$$
\boxed{\lambda_1=1,\qquad\lambda_2=2+a.}
$$
They are distinct and positive, with <eigenvectors> $(1,-1)$ and $(a,1)$ respectively. Thus **the linearized <critical point> is an <unstable node>**. It is a <hyperbolic equilibrium>, so the original nonlinear <equilibrium point> is also locally repelling; this last observation is stronger than the requested linearized classification.
